# TypeScript professional standards

## Policy

- **New and modified code** must use accurate types. Do **not** add new `any`, `as any`, or `@ts-ignore` except with a one-line comment and ticket/issue reference.
- The repo still has legacy `any`. Do **not** mass-refactor it unless the user asks for a dedicated typing pass. When you edit a file, **improve types in the code you touch** (props, returns, callbacks, API payloads).
- Goal: eventual **strict, professional** typing across frontend, backend, and shared — one source of truth per concept, **no duplicated type definitions**.

## Where types live (single source of truth)

| Concern | Location |
|---------|----------|
| Cross-app domain (Load, Company, Role, permissions, status enums) | `shared/` → `@tripsheet/shared` |
| Frontend app shell (nav, routing, session shape) | `frontend/src/types/` |
| Frontend feature-only (UI state, tab props, view models) | `frontend/src/features/<domain>/types.ts` or `types/` |
| HTTP request bodies / query | Backend `dto/*.dto.ts` (class-validator) |
| Service-internal helpers | Colocate `*.types.ts` in the same Nest module |

**Rules:** Import domain models from `@tripsheet/shared`. Do not redefine the same entity in frontend and backend. Map API/Prisma shapes to shared types at boundaries when fields differ.

## Practices (apply on every change)

- Explicit **props and hook return types**; extract `interface XxxProps` / `type XxxResult` when non-trivial.
- **`import type`** for type-only imports; enable strict inference (avoid generics that collapse to `{ id?: string }`-style widows — use shared models like `Load`).
- Prefer **`unknown`** over `any` for untrusted data; narrow with guards or validators (DTOs on backend).
- Prefer **`satisfies`**, `Pick` / `Omit` / `Partial` on shared types over copy-pasted field lists.
- API layer: type `lib/api.ts` methods and feature fetchers; avoid `(data: any)`.
- React: typed event handlers (`ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>`), discriminated unions for UI state.
- Do not use type assertions to silence errors — fix the model, add a mapper, or extend shared types once.

## Quick examples

```typescript
// ❌ Avoid
export function Panel({ user, loads }: any) { ... }

// ✅ Prefer
import type { Load } from '@tripsheet/shared';
import type { SessionUser } from '@/types/app';

export interface PanelProps {
  user: SessionUser;
  loads: Load[];
}
export function Panel({ user, loads }: PanelProps) { ... }
```

Build shared after changing `shared/`: `cd shared && npm run build`.
